WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.967
Encapsulation.

2
00:00:02.267 --> 00:00:05.964
Python trusts you, but it still lets a class guard its own data.

3
00:00:05.964 --> 00:00:06.600
Here's how.

4
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.733
Python has no private keyword.

5
00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:14.733
Instead, a leading underscore says: this is internal, touch it at your own risk.

6
00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:18.067
And for real protection, there's the property decorator.

7
00:00:18.367 --> 00:00:22.733
Callers see a plain attribute, while your validation sits invisibly behind it.

8
00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:25.333
Here's how the gate works.

9
00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:28.800
A caller assigns to what looks like a normal attribute.

10
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.667
Python quietly routes that through the setter, which checks the value.

11
00:00:32.967 --> 00:00:36.333
Valid values pass through into the internal attribute.

12
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:39.033
Bad values bounce back as a ValueError.

13
00:00:40.233 --> 00:00:42.600
Why not write getters and setters like Java?

14
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:46.000
Python starts with a plain attribute, no boilerplate.

15
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.233
Callers always just read and write the attribute.

16
00:00:49.533 --> 00:00:53.667
So next month you can add a guard, and not a single caller needs to change.

17
00:00:54.867 --> 00:00:57.500
Here's a cryostat that cools a space telescope.

18
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:02.600
Even the constructor assigns through the setter, so validation applies from the start.

19
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:06.100
The getter simply returns the internal underscore value.

20
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.500
And the setter refuses anything below absolute zero.

21
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.000
Here's a guard saving a real project.

22
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:19.433
A RAG service exposes chunk size as a property, allowed from one hundred to two thousand tokens.

23
00:01:19.733 --> 00:01:22.233
A teammate sets it to zero in an experiment.

24
00:01:22.533 --> 00:01:27.733
They get an immediate, readable error, instead of silently poisoning retrieval for a week.

25
00:01:28.933 --> 00:01:29.809
Let's recap.

26
00:01:29.809 --> 00:01:31.633
Privacy is by convention.

27
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:34.800
Property hides a guard behind a plain attribute.

28
00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.667
You can upgrade later without changing callers.

29
00:01:37.967 --> 00:01:40.412
So skip the getter and setter ritual.

30
00:01:40.412 --> 00:01:41.800
Next up: inheritance.
